# Line endings.
#
# Python sources are stored with LF endings in the repository and checked out
# with LF on every platform. tools/validate/validate.py was once committed with
# CRLF endings by a merge, which made every change to it conflict across the
# whole file; this rule keeps that from happening again.
#
# Only *.py is covered on purpose. Fixture files (*.toml), conformance vectors,
# evidence captures and archives are never rewritten: their bytes are hashed
# (pack digests, registry entries, archive checksums), so they get no text
# normalization from git.
*.py text eol=lf

# The files whose bytes are hashed are checked out with LF on every platform,
# for the same reason: their bytes end up in a pack digest, a registry entry
# or an archive checksum.
#
# This needs an explicit rule. Without one, git's core.autocrlf (true is the
# default on the Windows runners) rewrites LF as CRLF in a checkout, so the
# archive built on Windows differs from the one built on Linux or macOS even
# though nothing in the repository changed. That was measured, not assumed:
# see docs/archive-reproducibility.md.
protocol-*/** text eol=lf
tests/vectors/** text eol=lf
